\begin{abstract}
The Vietoris--Rips construction assigns to a metric space and a scale parameter a graph
whose edges record proximity. We isolate and formalize the precise order-theoretic content
of this construction on \emph{finite} metric spaces, packaging the size of the Rips
$1$-skeleton as a monotone integer-valued \emph{edge-count profile}
$\profile : \N \to \N$. Our central results establish that this profile is
(i) monotone non-decreasing in the threshold, (ii) zero at threshold zero in any metric
space, and (iii) uniformly bounded above by the number of unordered point-pairs. We show
that monotonicity is precisely the property that allows the assignment
\emph{finite metric space} $\mapsto$ \emph{edge-count profile} to be read as a functor into
an ordered, idempotent (``tropical'') target---the natural home of valuation-like,
order-preserving summaries. The arguments reduce, by design, to two robust primitives:
subgraph monotonicity of the Rips graph under threshold increase, and monotonicity of
cardinality under set inclusion. We then develop a companion \emph{dynamical bridge}: a
formal theory of continuous iteration showing that iterates of a continuous self-map remain
continuous, that orbit maps into finite product spaces are continuous, that compactness and
connectedness are transported by iteration, and that commutation and semiconjugacy
intertwine all iterates. Both developments instantiate a single thesis: the load-bearing
theorems of applied geometry and dynamics are statements that structure is carried
faithfully by monotone, structure-preserving maps. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
Topological data analysis (TDA) reconstructs qualitative shape from finite samples. Its
foundational device is the \emph{filtration}: a one-parameter family of combinatorial
objects, indexed by a scale, that grows monotonically with the parameter. The
Vietoris--Rips filtration is the most widely used such family. From a finite metric space
$(\alpha, \dist)$ and a scale $\varepsilon \in \R$, it builds the \textbf{Rips graph} whose
vertices are the points of $\alpha$ and whose edges join pairs within distance
$\varepsilon$. As $\varepsilon$ increases, edges appear and never disappear; the
\emph{scales of appearance and disappearance} of homological features constitute the
\emph{persistence diagram}, the principal invariant of TDA.

The entire interpretive scaffolding of persistence---birth scales, death scales, barcodes,
stability---depends on a single structural fact: \textbf{monotonicity}. A feature, once
present, must remain present at larger scales, for otherwise ``birth before death'' would be
meaningless. This paper isolates that fact at the level of the $1$-skeleton, formalizes it
together with its natural numerical avatar (the edge count), and exhibits the resulting
assignment as a functor into an ordered tropical target.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{The edge-count profile} (Definition~\ref{def:profile}): a canonical,
  finiteness-robust integer summary $\profile : \N \to \N$ of the Rips $1$-skeleton, defined
  via $\ncard$ of the edge set so that statements carry no \texttt{Fintype}/\texttt{Decidable}
  side conditions.
  \item \textbf{Monotonicity} (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} and Corollary~\ref{cor:Mono}): the
  profile is non-decreasing, packaged both as a pointwise inequality and as the
  order-theoretic statement $\mathrm{Monotone}(\profile)$.
  \item \textbf{Boundary and bound} (Theorems~\ref{thm:zero} and~\ref{thm:bound}): the
  profile vanishes at threshold zero in any metric space, and is uniformly bounded by the
  number of unordered pairs $\#\,\Sym_2 \alpha$.
  \item \textbf{The functorial bridge} (Section~\ref{sec:functor}): an account of why
  monotonicity is exactly the property making \emph{space} $\mapsto$ \emph{profile} a functor
  into an idempotent, order-theoretic (tropical) target.
  \item \textbf{A dynamical companion bridge} (Section~\ref{sec:dyn}): a formal theory of
  continuous iteration.
\end{enumerate}
All statements are presented with proof sketches; each has been fully formally verified.

\section{Preliminaries}

\subsection{Finite metric and pseudometric spaces}
A \textbf{pseudometric space} is a set $\alpha$ with $\dist : \alpha \times \alpha \to \R$
satisfying $\dist\,x\,x = 0$, symmetry, the triangle inequality, and non-negativity
$0 \le \dist\,x\,y$. A \textbf{metric space} additionally satisfies
$\dist\,x\,y = 0 \Rightarrow x = y$. We work with a finite type $\alpha$ carrying a metric;
finiteness of $\alpha$ makes the set of unordered pairs $\Sym_2 \alpha$ finite.

\subsection{Simple graphs and edge sets}
A simple graph on $\alpha$ is given by an irreflexive symmetric adjacency relation
$\mathrm{Adj}$. Its \textbf{edge set} $\edgeSet \subseteq \Sym_2 \alpha$ consists of the
unordered pairs $s(x,y)$ with $x$ adjacent to $y$. Graphs are ordered by
$G \le H \iff \forall x\,y,\ G.\mathrm{Adj}\,x\,y \to H.\mathrm{Adj}\,x\,y$, and this order
is reflected on edge sets: $G \le H \Rightarrow \edgeSet(G) \subseteq \edgeSet(H)$.

\subsection{Counting with $\ncard$}
For a set $s$, $\ncard\,s \in \N$ is its natural-number cardinality ($0$ if infinite). We
use: if $s \subseteq t$ and $t$ is finite, then $\ncard\,s \le \ncard\,t$. Using $\ncard$
(rather than $\mathrm{Finset.card}$) keeps the statements free of finiteness hypotheses;
finiteness is supplied on demand from $\alpha$ finite.

\section{The Rips Graph and the Edge-Count Profile}

\begin{definition}[Rips graph]\label{def:rips}
For a pseudometric space $\alpha$ and $\varepsilon \in \R$, the \textbf{Rips graph}
$\rips\,\alpha\,\varepsilon$ has adjacency
\[
  (\rips\,\alpha\,\varepsilon).\mathrm{Adj}\,x\,y
  \iff x \ne y \ \wedge\ \dist\,x\,y \le \varepsilon .
\]
Symmetry follows from $\dist\,x\,y = \dist\,y\,x$; irreflexivity from the $x \ne y$ clause.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Graph monotonicity]\label{lem:gmono}
If $\varepsilon_1 \le \varepsilon_2$ then
$\rips\,\alpha\,\varepsilon_1 \le \rips\,\alpha\,\varepsilon_2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $x,y$ be adjacent at scale $\varepsilon_1$, so $x \ne y$ and
$\dist\,x\,y \le \varepsilon_1$. Then $\dist\,x\,y \le \varepsilon_1 \le \varepsilon_2$, so
$x,y$ are adjacent at scale $\varepsilon_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Vanishing at zero in a metric space]\label{lem:zero}
In a metric space, $\rips\,\alpha\,0 = \bot$ (the edgeless graph).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $x,y$ were adjacent at scale $0$ then $\dist\,x\,y \le 0$, and with $0 \le \dist\,x\,y$
this gives $\dist\,x\,y = 0$, hence $x = y$, contradicting $x \ne y$. The converse is
vacuous.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Vanishing at negative scale]\label{lem:neg}
In a pseudometric space, $\varepsilon < 0 \Rightarrow \rips\,\alpha\,\varepsilon = \bot$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Adjacency would require $\dist\,x\,y \le \varepsilon < 0$, contradicting
$0 \le \dist\,x\,y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Edge-count profile]\label{def:profile}
For a finite metric space $\alpha$, define
\[
  \profile\,\alpha : \N \to \N, \qquad
  \profile\,\alpha\,r := \ncard\bigl(\edgeSet(\rips\,\alpha\,(r : \R))\bigr).
\]
That is, $\profile\,\alpha\,r$ is the number of edges of the Rips graph at the real
threshold obtained by casting the integer $r$.
\end{definition}

\section{Main Results}

\begin{theorem}[Edge-count monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
For all $r, s \in \N$ with $r \le s$,
$\profile\,\alpha\,r \le \profile\,\alpha\,s$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Cast $r \le s$ to $(r:\R) \le (s:\R)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:gmono},
$\rips\,\alpha\,r \le \rips\,\alpha\,s$, hence
$\edgeSet(\rips\,\alpha\,r) \subseteq \edgeSet(\rips\,\alpha\,s)$. The larger edge set is
finite (it sits in $\Sym_2 \alpha$), so $\ncard$ of the subset is at most $\ncard$ of the
superset.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Order-theoretic packaging]\label{cor:Mono}
$\mathrm{Monotone}(\profile\,\alpha)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Unfolding $\mathrm{Monotone}$, this is exactly Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero threshold]\label{thm:zero}
$\profile\,\alpha\,0 = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The cast of $0:\N$ is $0:\R$, so the graph is $\rips\,\alpha\,0 = \bot$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:zero}; the empty graph has empty edge set, of $\ncard$ equal to $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound by unordered pairs]\label{thm:bound}
For all $r \in \N$, $\profile\,\alpha\,r \le \#\,\Sym_2 \alpha$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The edge set is a subset of the universe of $\Sym_2 \alpha$. Since $\Sym_2 \alpha$ is
finite, $\ncard(\edgeSet) \le \ncard(\mathrm{univ}) = \#\,\Sym_2 \alpha$.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:mono},~\ref{thm:zero}, and~\ref{thm:bound} jointly characterize the
profile as a non-decreasing staircase function on $\N$, pinned to $0$ at the origin and
capped at $\#\,\Sym_2 \alpha = n(n-1)/2$ for $n = \#\,\alpha$.

\section{The Functorial Bridge to Tropical Valuation Objects}\label{sec:functor}

\subsection{Objects: monotone profiles as valuation objects}
Let $\mathrm{Prof}$ denote the set of monotone functions $\N \to \N$ that vanish at $0$ and
are bounded. By Corollary~\ref{cor:Mono} and Theorems~\ref{thm:zero}--\ref{thm:bound},
every finite metric space produces an element of $\mathrm{Prof}$. We regard $\mathrm{Prof}$
as a \emph{tropical valuation object}: it is partially ordered pointwise
($p \le q \iff \forall r,\ p\,r \le q\,r$), and the relevant algebra is idempotent---the
operations governing accumulation of features are $\max$ and $\le$, not $+$ and $\times$. A
monotone, non-decreasing, bounded integer staircase is precisely a discrete \emph{valuation}.

\subsection{Morphisms: non-expanding maps and profile domination}
The natural morphisms between metric spaces are \textbf{non-expanding} maps $f$ with
$\dist(f\,x, f\,y) \le \dist\,x\,y$. Such a map carries proximity forward: if $x,y$ are
adjacent in $\rips\,\alpha\,\varepsilon$, then $\dist(f\,x, f\,y) \le \varepsilon$. For
\emph{injective} non-expanding maps, the induced map on edge sets is injective, and counting
yields a \textbf{domination} of profiles,
\[
  \profile\,\alpha\,r \le \profile\,\beta\,r \qquad \text{for all } r,
\]
which we call \texttt{RipsProfileDomination}.

\subsection{Functoriality}
The assignment $F : (\text{space}) \mapsto \profile$ on objects and
$F : (\text{injective non-expanding map}) \mapsto (\text{domination})$ on morphisms respects
identities and composition, hence behaves as a functor from finite metric spaces with
injective non-expanding maps into the thin category $(\mathrm{Prof}, \le)$. Because the
target is a partial order, the morphism between two objects is unique when it exists.
Domination is reflexive and transitive (a preorder); on the quotient by ``equal profile''
it is antisymmetric, giving a genuine partial order---the $\le$-antisymmetry axiom of the
ambient tropical valuation object.

\subsection{The profile as a discrete derivative}
The increment $\profile\,\alpha\,r - \profile\,\alpha\,(r-1)$ counts the pairs whose
distance lies in $(r-1, r]$. Hence the profile is a cumulative count of the binned
pairwise-distance histogram, and equal profiles for all $r$ is equivalent to an equal
multiset of binned pairwise distances.

\section{The Dynamical Companion Bridge: Continuous Iteration}\label{sec:dyn}

\begin{theorem}[Iterates are continuous]\label{thm:iter}
If $f : \alpha \to \alpha$ is continuous, then for every $n \in \N$ the iterate $f^{[n]}$ is
continuous.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction: $f^{[0]} = \mathrm{id}$, and $f^{[n+1]} = f \circ f^{[n]}$ is a composite of
continuous maps.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Orbit map continuity]\label{thm:orbit}
For continuous $f$ and fixed $N$, the orbit map
$x \mapsto (k \mapsto f^{[k]}\,x) : \alpha \to (\mathrm{Fin}\,N \to \alpha)$ is continuous.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A map into a product is continuous iff each coordinate is; coordinate $k$ is $f^{[k]}$,
continuous by Theorem~\ref{thm:iter}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Compactness transport]\label{thm:cpt}
If $f$ is continuous and $s$ is compact, then $f^{[n]} \im s$ is compact for every $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The continuous image of a compact set is compact; apply with $f^{[n]}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Connectedness transport]\label{thm:conn}
If $f$ is continuous and $s$ is connected, then $f^{[n]} \im s$ is connected for every $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The continuous image of a connected set is connected; apply with $f^{[n]}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semiconjugacy intertwines iterates]\label{thm:semi}
If $h \circ f = g \circ h$, then $h \circ f^{[n]} = g^{[n]} \circ h$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$; the step uses
$h \circ f^{[n+1]} = (g^{[n]} \circ h) \circ f = g^{[n]} \circ (g \circ h)
 = g^{[n+1]} \circ h$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Commuting maps pass through iterates]\label{thm:comm}
If $f \circ g = g \circ f$, then $g \circ f^{[n]} = f^{[n]} \circ g$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Set-level transfer]\label{thm:settransfer}
If $f$ and $g$ commute, then for every set $s$ and every $n$,
$g \im (f^{[n]} \im s) = f^{[n]} \im (g \im s)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$g \im (f^{[n]} \im s) = (g \circ f^{[n]}) \im s = (f^{[n]} \circ g) \im s
 = f^{[n]} \im (g \im s)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:comm}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Continuous semiconjugate orbit map]\label{thm:semiorbit}
If $f, g, h$ are continuous and $h \circ f = g \circ h$, then for fixed $N$ the map
$x \mapsto (k \mapsto g^{[k]}(h\,x))$ is continuous.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
It is the composite of $h$ with the $g$-orbit map, continuous by
Theorem~\ref{thm:orbit}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Edge-count profile evaluation}
Given a distance matrix $D$ on $n$ points and threshold $r$, count unordered pairs within
distance $r$ in $O(n^2)$; the full profile over $0..R$ costs $O(R\,n^2)$ by independent
sweeps or $O(n^2 \log n)$ by sorting the pairwise distances once and accumulating.

\subsection{Orbit-vector evaluation}
Given $f$ and horizon $N$, iterate $f$ from $x$, recording each intermediate value, in
$O(N \cdot \mathrm{cost}(f))$; this is the constructive content of Theorem~\ref{thm:orbit}.

\section{Applications}
Persistent homology ($H_0$ and the $1$-skeleton); compact, comparable shape fingerprints
amenable to tropical (order/$\max$) composition; recovery of the binned pairwise-distance
histogram; and observable dynamics via continuous orbit embeddings with certified transport
of compactness, connectedness, and symmetry.

\section{Discussion}
The mathematical heart of this work is deliberately elementary---a one-line inequality and
the monotonicity of cardinality under inclusion---but its significance is structural. The
$\ncard$-based formulation keeps statements free of finiteness side conditions, and the
functorial reading connects metric geometry, tropical order theory, and computation. The
dynamical companion reinforces the same principle in a different category.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Strict monotonicity across critical scales]
For a finite metric space with two points at distance $d$, the profile strictly increases at
$r = \lceil d \rceil$ whenever a pair first becomes connected there. The weak inequality is
Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}; the strict version needs an explicit witnessing edge.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Profiles separate spaces up to distance multiset]
Two finite metric spaces with integer distances have equal profiles for all $r$ iff they
have the same multiset of pairwise distances.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Domination is a partial order]
On the quotient by ``equal profile'', \texttt{RipsProfileDomination} is antisymmetric:
mutual domination forces equal profiles.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Reversed bound for non-injective maps]
A surjective non-expanding map $f : \alpha \to \beta$ satisfies
$\profile\,\beta\,r \le (\text{a fiber-dependent factor}) \cdot \profile\,\alpha\,r$;
quotient maps can only decrease edges after accounting for collapsed fibers.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}
We have formalized the order-theoretic core of the Vietoris--Rips construction on finite
metric spaces. The edge-count profile is a monotone, boundary-pinned, uniformly bounded
integer staircase, and monotonicity realizes \emph{space} $\mapsto$ \emph{profile} as a
functor into an idempotent tropical valuation object. A companion theory of continuous
iteration demonstrates the same structural principle in topological dynamics. All results
are verified in full.

